<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<RunSettings>

  <!--
    Coverage collection settings for the CRAP (Change Risk Anti-Patterns) score.
    Consumed by scripts/Measure-Crap.ps1; see docs/crap.md for what the numbers mean.

    🔴 COBERTURA IS THE FORMAT THE CRAP SCORE COMES FROM. This is the opposite of
    the usual advice, and it was MEASURED on this repository (ReportGenerator
    5.5.11 + coverlet 6.0.4), not assumed:

      - coverlet's Cobertura carries per-method `complexity`, and ReportGenerator
        COMPUTES the Crap Score from it. It has done so since 5.2.1 ("Added 'Crap
        Score' metric for Cobertura coverage files" in its own changelog).
      - coverlet's OpenCover carries `cyclomaticComplexity` and `nPathComplexity`
        but NOT the `crapScore` attribute. ReportGenerator's OpenCover parser
        READS that attribute rather than computing anything, because the real
        OpenCover tool emits it. Coverlet does not - so an OpenCover-only run
        produces a Risk Hotspots table with Cyclomatic and NPath columns and NO
        Crap Score column at all: a green run with the one number missing.

    OpenCover is still collected beside it, because it is the only source of the
    NPath complexity metric and costs one extra file per suite. Nothing reads it
    for CRAP.

    scripts/Measure-Crap.ps1 does not trust any of the above. It asserts the
    Cobertura files carry per-method complexity BEFORE rendering, and asserts the
    rendered report really does contain a Crap Score column AFTER - which is the
    check that would have caught this inversion on day one.
  -->

  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura,opencover</Format>

          <!--
            Every suite under tests/ is named SlayIdleRepeat.<Area>.Tests, so the
            one filter below matches all nine. There are deliberately no
            `*.IntegrationTests` or `*.TestUtilities` filters: this repository has
            no integration tier ($noIntegrationTier in build/ci/test-suites.json)
            and no shared test-helper assembly — tests/Shared/ is linked source,
            not a project. A filter that matches nothing is dead config that reads
            as protection, which is the same failure mode rule 4 of
            test-suites.json exists to prevent.

            This is belt-and-braces over IncludeTestAssembly=false below; that
            setting excludes the assembly under collection, this excludes a test
            assembly reached as a reference from another one.
          -->
          <Exclude>[*.Tests]*</Exclude>

          <ExcludeByAttribute>Obsolete,GeneratedCodeAttribute,CompilerGeneratedAttribute,ExcludeFromCodeCoverageAttribute</ExcludeByAttribute>

          <!--
            🔴 `**/*.generated.cs` is what keeps Godot.SourceGenerators out of
            the ranking. Without it the entire top of the CRAP list is
            InvokeGodotClassMethod / Get- and SetGodotClassPropertyValue /
            RestoreGodotObjectData - generated dispatch switches with cyclomatic
            complexity up to 92 that nobody wrote and nobody can refactor, and
            they crowd out every hand-written method. The `-*Generated*` class
            filter passed to ReportGenerator does NOT catch them: it matches on
            the CLASS name, and these sit on ordinary classes like
            Game.Scenes.Board.

            No `**/Migrations/*.cs` entry, for the reason given above: there is no
            EF Core migration anywhere in this tree (the Postgres adapter carries
            none). Add it the day one appears; do not carry it speculatively.
          -->
          <ExcludeByFile>**/*.generated.cs,**/*.g.cs,**/*.Designer.cs</ExcludeByFile>

          <IncludeTestAssembly>false</IncludeTestAssembly>

          <!--
            Auto-properties are complexity 1 and either fully covered or not
            touched. Including them buries the methods that actually carry risk
            under hundreds of trivial rows.
          -->
          <SkipAutoProps>true</SkipAutoProps>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>

</RunSettings>
